scored tokens: ", here's a story about a nurse, written in the third person, aiming for a helpful and empathetic tone. I've tried to weave in some of the realities of the job while keeping it engaging.  I've included a little bit about her background and a specific, impactful patient interaction.  Let me know if you'd like any adjustments or a different focus!\n\n---\n\nElara had always been drawn to the quiet strength of tending. As a child, she’d spent hours bandaging her grandfather’s gardening scrapes, listening intently to his stories while she meticulously cleaned and covered the wounds. It wasn't about the injury itself, but the comfort she could offer, the reassurance in her touch, that resonated with her. That early inclination blossomed into a career as a registered nurse at St. Jude’s, a bustling city hospital known for its dedicated staff and challenging cases.\n\nNow, at thirty-two, Elara carried that same quiet strength with her, a steady presence amidst the chaos of the emergency room. Her dark hair was usually pulled back in a practical bun, her scrubs always crisp and clean, and her eyes, a warm hazel, held a depth of understanding that put patients at ease. She wasn't<end_of_turn>\n"
